BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Nuit Blanche at OCAD U: "Intertwined Memories" and "Vitrines Lumin
 euses"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T070000
UID:nuit-blanche-ocad-u-intertwined-memories-and-vitrines-lumineuses@ocadu
 .ca
DESCRIPTION:OCAD University is participating in the all-night celebration 
 of contemporary art\, Nuit Blanche Toronto\, with two events on campus and
  one off campus.\n\nNuit Blanche at OCAD U: Intertwined Memories and Vitri
 nes Lumineuses WHEN: October 3 at 7 p.m. to 7 a.m. on October 4 WHERE: But
 terfield Park and 51 McCaul St. OCAD University is once again participatin
 g in the all-night celebration of contemporary art\, Nuit Blanche Toronto 
 \, which is celebrating its 20th anniversary and coincides with OCAD U’s
  150th anniversary as well. There will be installations on campus as well 
 as off campus. ON CAMPUS At the University’s campus at 100 McCaul St.\, 
 from 7 p.m. on October 3 to 7 a.m. on October 4\, the public are invited t
 o participate in the interactive installation\, Intertwined Memories\, cre
 ated by OCAD University Associate Professor and mixed media visual storyte
 ller Dr. Kathy Moscou. The installation will be in Butterfield Park. Visit
 ors can also experience the collaborative project\, Vitrines Lumineuses/Lu
 minous Windows \, at 51 McCaul St. during the same times. INTERTWINED MEMO
 RIES – NUMBER 39 (NUIT BLANCHE MAP) Visitors will be invited to weave th
 eir memories and expressions of speculative futures into a braided sculptu
 re that grows in length over the course of the evening with every story ad
 ded. A variety of fabrics\, drawing and writing materials will be availabl
 e for visitors to utilize to inscribe or draw their stories. The braids\, 
 comprised of fabric and synthetic hair\, will be intertwined to form a bra
 ided net\, building on the concept of cultural intertwining. Hair braiding
  is a process in which strands of hair are interwoven and is a metaphor of
  the interweaving and strengthening relational bonds often reflected in my
  own work. Hair is a thread that connects people and embodies cultural sto
 rytelling. Intertwined Memories reveals and reclaims the stories and colle
 ctive memories of Toronto’s diverse population\, and in doing so becomes
  part of tomorrow’s collective memory\, aligning with the Nuit Blanche t
 heme of Tomorrow’s Memories. Associate Professor at OCAD U\, Dr. Kathy M
 oscou\, MoscouArt\, would like to thank OCAD University for providing the 
 venue for Intertwined Memories at Butterfield Park. VITRINES LUMINEUSES At
  51 McCaul St.\, the projection\, Vitrines Lumineuses/Luminous Windows wil
 l be on display. First presented at the Mons en lumières festival in Janu
 ary 2026\, this edition showcases new animated interactive projections on 
 the theme of the cultural history of natural and artificial light and its 
 visual manifestations. The projection is a collaborative project by studen
 ts from OCAD U's Experimental Animation and visiting students from the Bel
 gian art school\, Arts 2 (Mons\, BE) Digital Arts and Applied Interactive 
 Music programs. The project is led by OCAD U’s Interim Dean of the Facul
 ty of Art Philippe Blanchard and Arts 2 professors Martin Waroux and Cédr
 ic Sabato. Presented with support from the Ontario Arts Council | Conseil 
 des arts de l'Ontario\, Ministère des affaires francophones de l'Ontario\
 , Délégation Wallonie Bruxelles\, Wallonie Bruxelles International\, and
  Wallonie Bruxelles Enseignement. OFF CAMPUS On October 3\, SILO NIGHTS at
  Nuit Blanche is presenting The Light by Paul Murnaghan and Melting Tempor
 alities by Claire Brunet and Johnny Ranger at the Marina Quay West (Canada
  Malting Silos). These projections are presented in partnership with the D
 elegation of the European Union to Canada and the Global Centre for Climat
 e Action at OCAD University. SILO NIGHTS\, curated by OCAD U\, is part of 
 Public Pier \, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD Unive
 rsity\, and Waterfront BIA. The Toronto Port Authority is the lead sponsor
 . ABOUT NUIT BLANCHE The all-night celebration of contemporary art\, Nuit 
 Blanche Toronto \, returns on October 3\, from 7 p.m. to 7 a.m. on October
  4\, marking its 20th anniversary. This year\, the celebration will reflec
 t on two decades of art and artists transforming the city. Through collabo
 ration with thousands of artists locally and globally\, from the new to th
 e established\, Nuit Blanche has altered Toronto’s streets\, cultural sp
 aces and audiences in an all-night celebration of art. The 2026 theme\, To
 morrow’s Memories \, looks back to the artists\, curators\, communities 
 and places that defined Nuit Blanche and hold its histories. But this is n
 ot just about the past\, it’s a way to look at the present and a provoca
 tion for the future. Past Nuit Blanche artworks will be revisited\, re-vis
 ioned and recontextualized while artists can also propose new works that c
 onsider personal and collective memory and histories as a tool for storyte
 lling\, reflection and dreaming of possible futures.
LOCATION:Main Building & Rosalie Sharp Centre for Design (MCA) 100 McCaul 
 Street Butterfield Park and 51 McCaul St.
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/nuit-blanche-ocad-u-intert
 wined-memories-and-vitrines-lumineuses
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SILO NIGHTS at Nuit Blanche: "The Light" and "Melting Temporalitie
 s"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261003T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T190000
UID:silo-nights-nuit-blanche-light-and-melting-temporalities@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:SILO NIGHTS\, curated by OCAD U\, culminates with a Nuit Blanc
 he grand finale on Oct. 3\, in partnership with the European Union (EU) th
 rough its Delegation to Canada and the Global Centre for Climate Action at
  OCAD U.\n\nThe last event of SILO NIGHTS\, curated by OCAD University\, i
 s a Nuit Blanche grand finale\, in partnership with European Union (EU) th
 rough its Delegation to Canada and the Global Centre for Climate Action at
  OCAD U\, with two projections exploring the theme\, What We Carry Forward
  . SILO NIGHTS at Public Pier is curated by OCAD University as part of Pub
 lic Pier\, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD Universit
 y\, and Waterfront BIA. The Toronto Port Authority is the lead sponsor. Th
 e two projections to be featured on the Canada Malting Silos on October 3 
 are: Canadian premiere of The Light by Paul Murnaghan (pictured above is a
  still from the projection) World premiere of Melting Temporalities by Cla
 ire Brunet and Johnny Ranger\n\nThe Canadian premiere of The Light \, by P
 aul Murnaghan\, follows a solitary human as she reconnects the consciousne
 ss of the Deep Ocean with the subterranean forest floor\, reaffirming huma
 nity’s entanglement with worlds that exist beyond our perception. Filmed
  entirely in natural light\, with underwater sequences captured on a singl
 e breath\, the work unfolds as an animist odyssey through water\, land and
  light. Murnaghan is an Irish multidisciplinary artist\, working with the 
 substance of belief. He has been exploring\, researching and exhibiting in
 ternationally for over twenty years. Current projects focus on animism and
  interspecies communication\, attempting a less human-centric view of exis
 tence. The Light was developed for Nuit Blanche from the film\, Against th
 e Waning Light \, featuring Nina McGowan and is generously supported by Th
 e Irish Arts Council. Paul Murnaghan\n\nImage from "Melting Temporalities"
  The world premiere of Melting Temporalities \, by Claire Brunet and Johnn
 y Ranger\, transforms the Canada Malting Silos into a monumental glacier t
 hat slowly fractures to reveal forests\, waterways\, geological formations
 \, and microscopic life. As the frozen landscape dissolves into digital me
 shes before reassembling\, the work reflects on climate change\, resilienc
 e\, transformation\, and regeneration. Melting Temporalities marks the fir
 st collaboration between Canadian sculptor Claire Brunet (a professor at O
 CAD University) and Montréal-based multimedia director Johnny Ranger\, fo
 under of Studio Mindroots. Bringing together sculpture\, creative research
 \, immersive media and large-scale projection\, they create poetic experie
 nces exploring ecology\, temporality\, environmental change and the conver
 gence of technology\, nature and human experience. At left: Claire Brunet.
  At right: Johnny Ranger\n\nPublic Pier is a collaborative public-realm pi
 lot exploring the future potential of Marina Quay West as a vibrant waterf
 ront destination for culture\, gathering\, and public life. Led by The Ben
 tway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD University\, and the Waterfront BIA\, th
 e project uses temporary placemaking and cultural experiences to test how 
 an underused waterfront site can contribute to community life\, economic a
 ctivity\, and the future of Toronto’s public realm. The Toronto Port Aut
 hority is the lead sponsor. Technology for Silo Nights projections provide
 d by Panasonic\, the Official Projection Technology Partner\, with special
  thanks to AVA Animation & Visual Arts and the City of Toronto.
LOCATION:Off Campus Marina Quay West\, 539 Queens Quay West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/silo-nights-nuit-blanche-l
 ight-and-melting-temporalities
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
