BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:SILO NIGHTS at Public Pier: "Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Trav
 el Diary of a Particle"
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T230000
UID:travel-diary@ocadu.ca
DESCRIPTION:SILO NIGHTS at Public Pier is curated by OCAD University as pa
 rt of the Public Pier\, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, O
 CAD University\, and Waterfront BIA with the Toronto Port Authority as lea
 d sponsor.\n\nSILO NIGHTS AT PUBLIC PIER\, CURATED BY OCAD UNIVERSITY For 
 six nights only\, Toronto’s waterfront lights up after dark. Starting Se
 ptember 18\, SILO NIGHTS transforms the iconic Canada Malting Silos into a
  giant canvas for immersive art and storytelling — featuring internation
 al artists\, student collaborations\, and Canadian and world premieres. SI
 LO NIGHTS at Public Pier is curated by OCAD University as part of Public P
 ier \, presented by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD University\, 
 and Waterfront BIA with the Toronto Port Authority as lead sponsor. On Fri
 day\, October 2\, from 8 to 11 p.m.\, SILO NIGHTS showcases the Toronto pr
 emiere of Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle . To
 ronto premiere Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle
  Presented with support from the Ontario Arts Council | Conseil des arts d
 e l'Ontario\, Ministère des affaires francophones de l'Ontario\, Déléga
 tion Wallonie Bruxelles\, Wallonie Bruxelles International\, and Wallonie 
 Bruxelles Enseignement. The Toronto premiere of Carnet de voyage d’une p
 articule/Travel Diary of a Particle is a 7-minute animated journey followi
 ng a photon from the sun to earth\, through space and underwater environme
 nts. Exploring light\, nature and ecology\, the project brings together st
 udents from OCAD University’s Experimental Animation program and student
 s from two ARTS² programs in Mons\, Belgium: Digital Arts within Visual A
 rts and Applied and Interactive Music within the Royal Conservatory of Mus
 ic. Students: OCAD University / 2D creation : Shannon Halliday\, Sophia Po
 aps\, Jae Despi\, Laura Barbi\, Aklesiya Yared\, Zahrah Khurram. Students:
  ARTS² / 3D creation: Mathias Serwy\, Natasha Alves de Arujo\, Noah Loute
 \, Lalie Istasse\, Julian Petit\, Eugene Kamegneson Fotso\, Clément Dandr
 imont\, Adriano Spadafora. Students: ARTS² / Sound creation: Oriane Quert
 enmont \, Romain Soudon \, William Escande \, Guillaume Toussaint \, Hugo 
 Drouillon \, Susy Magnien \, Noé Werlen \, Eliza Canivet Faculty: Philipp
 e Blanchard – OCAD University \; Martin Waroux – ARTS²\, Matthieu Buy
 taers – ARTS²\, Cédric Sabato – ARTS²\, Florent Schirrer – ARTS²
 . OCAD U Experimental Animation students with students from the Belgian ar
 t school\, ARTS2. Technology for Silo Nights provided by Panasonic\, the O
 fficial Projection Technology Partner\, with special thanks to AVA Animati
 on & Visual Arts and the City of Toronto. ABOUT PUBLIC PIER Public Pier is
  a collaborative public-realm pilot exploring the future potential of Mari
 na Quay West as a vibrant waterfront destination for culture\, gathering\,
  and public life. Led by The Bentway\, Harbourfront Centre\, OCAD Universi
 ty\, and the Waterfront BIA\, the project uses temporary placemaking and c
 ultural experiences to test how an underused waterfront site can contribut
 e to community life\, economic activity\, and the future of Toronto’s pu
 blic realm. The Toronto Port Authority is the lead sponsor. Visit Public P
 ier for the full schedule of programming. SILO NIGHTS AT PUBLIC PIER: PROG
 RAMMING SCHEDULE Friday\, September 18 and Saturday\, September 19 8 to 11
  p.m. World premiere Decomposition No. 7: Undergrowth by transmedia artist
  Ali Phi Curated and presented in partnership with The Bentway . This work
  imagines Toronto as a city shaped by what lies beneath it: root systems t
 hreading beneath the street grid\, connecting trees across the city\, and 
 holding the ground where new growth begins. This new immersive artwork rea
 ds the city's street-tree records\, including species\, trunk diameter\, a
 nd location\, as a score\, transforming data built for maintenance into li
 ght\, sound\, and a portrait of slow\, ongoing urban growth. Friday\, Sept
 ember 25 8 to 11 p.m. Canadian premiere Breathe (2026) by Faisal Anwar Pre
 sented in partnership with The Blackwood Gallery at the University of Toro
 nto Mississauga\, in conjunction with Faisal Anwar's CommonSky series feat
 ured in the exhibition Lines\, Pattern\, Action (September–December 2026
 ) at The Blackwood\, curated by Ruba Kana'an. In the Canadian premiere of 
 Breathe \, by Pakistani-Canadian hybrid artist Faisal Anwar \, the Purple 
 Martin’s 5\,000-mile migration from Brazil to North America transforms t
 he Canada Malting Silos into a living sky. Through generative visuals\, an
 imation\, sound\, and poetic storytelling\, the work follows the birds thr
 ough changing climates and disrupted ecologies. September 26 8 to 11 p.m. 
 World premiere The Shape of Memory by OOOPStudio The world premiere presen
 ted by Istituto Italiano di Cultura – Toronto\, Consulate General of Ita
 ly\, Cultural Section\, in collaboration with the European Union (EU) thro
 ugh its Delegation to Canada. The Shape of Memory treats a city's memory n
 ot as history to archive but as intelligence to activate\, the raw materia
 l from which communities build their next version of themselves. Projected
  on the Canada Malting Silos\, it returns Toronto's memory of land and wat
 er to its waterfront. The concept for The Shape of Memory was created by O
 OOPStudio\, with photogrammetry\, animation and editing by Alessandro Gris
 endi and 3D by Marco Noviello. September 26 8 to 11 p.m. LIving Waters\, W
 orld premiere of animation collaboration workshop project Presented in par
 tnership with the Embassy of France in Canada and Institut français du Ca
 nada\, in collaboration with the European Union (EU) through its Delegatio
 n to Canada. This is the world premiere of Living Waters\, resulting from 
 an animation collaboration workshop project led by French projection mappi
 ng artist and educator Pablo Gracias with OCAD University students in Expe
 rimental Animation and Graphic Design. October 2 8 to 11 p.m. Toronto prem
 iere Carnet de voyage d'une particule/Travel Diary of a Particle Presented
  with support from the Ontario Arts Council | Conseil des arts de l'Ontari
 o\, Ministère des affaires francophones de l'Ontario\, Délégation Wallo
 nie Bruxelles\, Wallonie Bruxelles International\, and Wallonie Bruxelles 
 Enseignement. Saturday\, October 3\, SILO NIGHTS at Nuit Blanche 7 p.m. to
  7 a.m. (October 4) Canadian premiere\, The Light World premiere\, Melting
  Temporalities Two artworks\, projected on the Canada Malting Silos \, wil
 l be unveiled for Nuit Blanche Toronto\, presented in partnership with the
  European Union (EU) through its Delegation to Canada and the Global Centr
 e for Climate Action at OCAD University. The Canadian premiere of The Ligh
 t \, by Paul Murnaghan\, follows a solitary human as she reconnects the co
 nsciousness of the Deep Ocean with the subterranean forest floor\, reaffir
 ming humanity’s entanglement with worlds that exist beyond our perceptio
 n. Filmed entirely in natural light\, with underwater sequences captured o
 n a single breath\, the work unfolds as an animist odyssey through water\,
  land\, and light. Murnaghan is an Irish multidisciplinary artist\, workin
 g with the substance of belief. He has been exploring\, researching and ex
 hibiting internationally for over twenty years. Current projects focus on 
 animism and interspecies communication\, attempting a less human-centric v
 iew of existence. The world premiere of Melting Temporalities \, by Claire
  Brunet and Johnny Ranger\, transforms the Canada Malting Silos into a mon
 umental glacier that slowly fractures to reveal forests\, waterways\, geol
 ogical formations\, and microscopic life. As the frozen landscape dissolve
 s into digital meshes before reassembling\, the work reflects on climate c
 hange\, resilience\, transformation\, and regeneration.
LOCATION:Off Campus Marina Quay West\, 539 Queens Quay West
URL:https://www.ocadu.ca/events-and-exhibitions/silo-nights-public-pier-ca
 rnet-de-voyage-dune-particule/travel-diary
X-SOURCE:OCAD University
END:VEVENT
